import Mathlib
import InverseGalois.Hilbert.GaloisAction

/-!
# Generic Polynomial and Symmetric Galois Groups

This file proves that the generic polynomial of degree n (with independent transcendental
coefficients) has Galois group Sₙ over its coefficient field. Combined with Hilbert's
irreducibility theorem, this gives an alternative proof that every Sₙ is an inverse
Galois group over ℚ.

## Main results

- `genericSplitPoly`: The polynomial ∏(X - xᵢ) ∈ ℚ[x₁,...,xₙ][X]
- `genericSplitPoly_coeff`: Its coefficients are elementary symmetric polynomials
- `sn_inverse_galois_via_generic`: Sₙ is inverse Galois (assuming HIT)

## Strategy

The key insight is that the splitting field of the generic polynomial is the field of
rational functions ℚ(x₁,...,xₙ) in n independent variables, where aᵢ = eᵢ(x₁,...,xₙ)
are the elementary symmetric polynomials. The Galois group is exactly Sₙ acting by
permuting the xᵢ.

## References

- Lang, S. "Algebra", Chapter V
- Hungerford, T. "Algebra", Section V.9
-/

open Polynomial MvPolynomial

noncomputable section

/-!
## Part 1: The generic polynomial as a product of linear factors

We work with the polynomial ring ℚ[x₁,...,xₙ] and construct the polynomial
∏(X - xᵢ) whose coefficients are the elementary symmetric polynomials.
-/

/-- The product polynomial ∏ᵢ (X - xᵢ) ∈ ℚ[x₁,...,xₙ][X], whose coefficients
are (up to sign) the elementary symmetric polynomials in x₁,...,xₙ.

This is the "universal" monic polynomial of degree n that splits completely
over ℚ(x₁,...,xₙ). -/
def genericSplitPoly (n : ℕ) : Polynomial (MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℚ) :=
  ∏ i : Fin n, (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C (MvPolynomial.X i))

/-- The generic polynomial is monic. -/
lemma genericSplitPoly_monic (n : ℕ) : (genericSplitPoly n).Monic := by
  unfold genericSplitPoly
  exact Polynomial.monic_prod_of_monic _ _ (fun i _ => Polynomial.monic_X_sub_C _)

/-
The generic polynomial has degree n.
-/
lemma genericSplitPoly_natDegree (n : ℕ) : (genericSplitPoly n).natDegree = n := by
  unfold genericSplitPoly
  simp [ Polynomial.natDegree_sub_eq_left_of_natDegree_lt ]

/-
The coefficients of the generic polynomial are elementary symmetric polynomials.
Specifically, the coefficient of X^(n-k) in ∏(X - xᵢ) is (-1)^k · eₖ(x₁,...,xₙ).
-/
lemma genericSplitPoly_coeff (n k : ℕ) (hk : k ≤ n) :
    (genericSplitPoly n).coeff (n - k) =
      (-1) ^ k * MvPolynomial.esymm (Fin n) ℚ k := by
        rw [ esymm ];
        erw [ genericSplitPoly, Finset.prod_congr rfl fun _ _ => sub_eq_add_neg _ _ ];
        rw [ Finset.prod_congr rfl fun _ _ => by rw [ ← Polynomial.C_neg ] ];
        erw [ Finset.prod_X_add_C_coeff ];
        · simp [ Nat.sub_sub_self hk, Finset.mul_sum _ _ _ ];
          exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun x hx => by rw [ Finset.prod_congr rfl fun _ _ => neg_eq_neg_one_mul _, Finset.prod_mul_distrib ] ; simp_all only [Finset.mem_powersetCard, Finset.subset_univ, true_and, Finset.prod_const];
        · simp +arith +decide

/-!
## Part 2: Galois theory of the generic polynomial

The splitting field of the generic polynomial over the field of symmetric
rational functions is the full rational function field ℚ(x₁,...,xₙ), with
Galois group Sₙ.

We formalize this by working with:
- The polynomial ring R = MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℚ
- The subalgebra S = symmetricSubalgebra (Fin n) ℚ of symmetric polynomials
- The fraction fields K = FractionRing R and F = FractionRing S
-/

/-
The Sₙ action on ℚ[x₁,...,xₙ] by permuting variables is faithful.
-/
lemma perm_action_faithful (n : ℕ) (hn : 2 ≤ n) :
    ∀ σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n),
      σ ≠ 1 → ∃ p : MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℚ,
        MvPolynomial.rename σ p ≠ p := by
          intro σ hσ
          obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ : ∃ i : Fin n, σ i ≠ i := by
            exact not_forall.mp fun h => hσ <| Equiv.ext h;
          refine' ⟨ MvPolynomial.X i, _ ⟩ ; simp_all [ MvPolynomial.rename_X ]

/-
The fixed field of the Sₙ action on ℚ(x₁,...,xₙ) is exactly ℚ(e₁,...,eₙ),
the field generated by the elementary symmetric polynomials.

This is the fundamental theorem of symmetric polynomials at the field level.
-/
lemma symmetric_fixed_field (n : ℕ) :
    ∀ p : MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℚ,
      (∀ σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n), MvPolynomial.rename σ p = p) →
        p ∈ symmetricSubalgebra (Fin n) ℚ := by
          unfold symmetricSubalgebra; intro p a
    simp_all only [Subalgebra.mem_mk, Subsemiring.mem_mk, Submonoid.mem_mk, Subsemigroup.mem_mk, Set.mem_setOf_eq]
    exact a;

/-!
## Part 3: Connection to Inverse Galois

Using the above results, we can give an alternative proof that Sₙ is an
inverse Galois group, assuming Hilbert's irreducibility theorem.
-/

/-- If Hilbert's irreducibility theorem holds (in a strong enough form), then
every Sₙ is an inverse Galois group over ℚ.

The assumption `hit` says: there exists an irreducible polynomial over ℚ of
degree n whose Galois group action on roots (via galActionHom) is bijective
(i.e., the Galois group is the full symmetric group). -/
theorem sn_inverse_galois_via_generic (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n)
    (hit : ∃ (f : ℚ[X]), Irreducible f ∧ f.natDegree = n ∧
      Function.Bijective (@Polynomial.Gal.galActionHom _ _ f ℂ _ _ ⟨IsAlgClosed.splits _⟩)) :
    IsInverseGalois (Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) := by
  obtain ⟨f, hf_irr, hf_deg, hf_bij⟩ := hit
  exact IsInverseGalois.of_galActionHom_bijective' n f hf_irr hf_deg hf_bij

end